<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jigawa State</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jigawa State is a state in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, created from part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kano-state">Kano State</a> in 1991, with its capital at Dutse. Largely flat savanna crossed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadejia">Hadejia</a> river, it is mainly agricultural. It includes traditional <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirates</a> such as Hadejia and Gumel and the old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> town of Garun Gabas, associated with the ancient kingdom of Biram.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jigawa-state.jpg" alt="Jigawa State" width="320" height="246" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Himalayan Explorer based on work by Uwe Dedering · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nigeria_Jigawa_State_map.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Jigawa State lies in the north-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, between latitudes 11°N and 13°N, and covers about 22,410 square kilometres. It borders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kano">Kano</a> and Katsina states to the west, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bauchi-state">Bauchi State</a> to the east and Yobe State to the north-east, and along its northern edge it shares a 70 km international border with the Zinder Region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a>. Its land is undulating, with sand dunes spanning several kilometres in places. The main rivers are the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadejia">Hadejia</a>, Kafin Hausa and Iggi, and the Hadejia–Kafin Hausa river crosses the state from west to east through the Hadejia-Nguru wetlands into the Lake Chad Basin. Most of the state lies within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> savannah.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The state is known for the Dutsen Habude cave paintings at Birnin Kudu, which have been dated to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> period. The town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadejia">Hadejia</a>, formerly Biram, is notable as one of the traditional seven true <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa states</a>. The area was formerly the north-eastern part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kano-state">Kano State</a>. Agitation for a separate state was led by Malam Inuwa-Dutse, a former commissioner in the ministry of agriculture and natural resources under Audu Bako, governor of the old Kano State. Jigawa was created on 27 August 1991 under the military administration of General Ibrahim Babangida.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The capital and largest city is Dutse, and the state has twenty-seven local government areas. The people are predominantly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a>, with Kanuri mainly in the Hadejia Emirate, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and a long history of intermarriage bind the three groups together. The state is governed under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharia">Sharia</a> law. Over 80% of the population is engaged in subsistence farming and animal husbandry, and trade is mostly small and medium in scale. The state has universities at Dutse, Babura, Kafin Hausa and Majia, and a federal polytechnic at Kazaure.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jigawa State” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It then became part of Northern Nigeria, and the Emir of Hadejia still reigns in <b>Jigawa State</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/hadejia-emirate">Hadejia Emirate</a></li><li><blockquote>Garun Gabas survives as a town in <b>Jigawa State</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-biram">Kingdom of Biram</a></li></ul></section>
